  "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-3$ and $k=1,$ so the center is located at $(-3,1).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=2$ and ${b}^{2}=4.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=2,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=4,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$",
